<div class="bg-zinc-950 text-zinc-100">
  <.marketing_nav current_user={@current_user} current={:docs} />

  <section class="py-12 sm:py-16">
    <.docs_layout
      current="quickstart"
      updated="August 26, 2026"
      toc={[
        {"create-account", "Create your account"},
        {"install-runner", "Install the runner"},
        {"watch-connect", "Watch it connect"},
        {"first-action", "Run your first action"},
        {"connect-llm", "Point your LLM at it"},
        {"workflow", "The workflow we recommend"}
      ]}
    >
      <.docs_header current="quickstart">
        <:dek>
          Connect one Linux host, run <code class="font-mono text-brand-300">linux.uptime</code>
          through policy, then point your LLM at the same catalog. About five minutes.
        </:dek>
      </.docs_header>

      <.docs_prerequisites>
        <:item>
          A Linux host with <strong class="text-zinc-300">systemd</strong>
          — a VM, a cloud instance, or bare metal. The installer sets up a systemd service; for
          containers, see <.link
            navigate={~p"/docs/containers"}
            class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
          >
                Install in a container</.link>.
        </:item>
        <:item><code class="text-zinc-300">sudo</code> on that host.</:item>
        <:item>
          Allow outbound HTTPS to <code class="text-zinc-300">emisar.dev:443</code>
          for the runner, installer, and release files, and to
          <code class="text-zinc-300">registry.emisar.dev:443</code>
          for action packs.
        </:item>
      </.docs_prerequisites>

      <.docs_h2 id="create-account">1. Create your account</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <.link href={~p"/sign_up"} class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300">Sign up</.link>
        to create your account. It's your control plane: runners connect to it, your LLM's MCP calls arrive there, and it applies your policy and writes the audit trail. The free plan covers <strong class="text-zinc-300">3 runners and 1 user</strong>. No credit card required.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="install-runner">2. Install the runner</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        The runner is a small service that lives on your host and executes actions there —
        nothing touches the host except through it. In the console, click <a
          href={~p"/app/runners/install"}
          class="font-medium text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/30 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
        >Runners → Connect a runner</a>. You'll get a one-line install command
        with a single-use enrollment key
        (<code class="font-mono text-brand-300">emkey-enroll-…</code>, shown <em>once</em>
        — copy it now). Run it on the host you want to give your LLM access to:
      </p>
      <.docs_code
        phx-no-format
        label="shell"
        copy_text="curl -fsSL https://emisar.dev/install.sh | sudo EMISAR_ENROLLMENT_KEY=emkey-enroll-… bash"
      ><span class="text-zinc-500">$</span> curl -fsSL https://emisar.dev/install.sh \
    | sudo EMISAR_ENROLLMENT_KEY=emkey-enroll-… bash</.docs_code>
      <.docs_verify_download tarball="emisar-<version>-linux-amd64.tar.gz" checksums="SHA256SUMS" />
      <.docs_install_script_details />
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        The installer also adds <strong class="text-zinc-300">starter packs</strong>. A pack is a set of ready-made actions for one tool
        — Linux basics, nginx, Postgres, etc. Pack actions are the tools your agent gets, so add more later
        for whatever else the host runs: see <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/use-a-published-pack"}
          class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
        >Use a published pack</.link>.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="watch-connect">3. Watch it connect</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        The console updates on its own: your host appears under <a
          href={~p"/app/runners"}
          class="font-medium text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/30 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
        >Runners</a>, online, advertising the actions it can run. You can also check from the host:
      </p>
      <.docs_code phx-no-format label="shell" copy_text="sudo emisar status"><span class="text-zinc-500">$</span> sudo emisar status

  ✓  connection    connected · heartbeat sent 3s ago
  ✓  catalog       4 packs · 82 actions advertised · all available
  ✓  process       PID 1842 · up 2m15s · 1 connection attempt(s)
  ✓  runs          0 in flight (last reported by the daemon)
  ✓  checks        7 local readiness checks passed

Runner is connected and healthy.</.docs_code>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        That is the host's own view. The console has the final say on whether the runner is
        really connected.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        The stock <code class="font-mono">linux-core</code>
        pack is trusted automatically because its hash matches the one emisar publishes. A
        custom or edited pack waits for a trust decision instead — that flow is in <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/pack-updates"}
          class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
        >Roll out and roll back packs</.link>.
      </p>
      <details class="group mt-6 overflow-hidden rounded-lg border border-zinc-900 bg-black/40">
        <summary class="flex cursor-pointer items-center justify-between gap-4 px-5 py-4 text-sm font-semibold text-zinc-100 transition-colors hover:bg-zinc-900/30 [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">
          <span class="flex items-center gap-2">
            <.icon name="story.debug" class="h-4 w-4 text-zinc-500" />
            The runner is not showing up
          </span>
          <.icon
            name="action.disclose"
            class="h-5 w-5 shrink-0 text-zinc-500 transition duration-200 group-hover:text-zinc-300 group-open:rotate-180 group-open:text-brand-400"
          />
        </summary>
        <div class="border-t border-zinc-900 px-5 pb-5 pt-4">
          <p class="text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
            Work through these on the host, in order:
          </p>
          <ul class="mt-3 space-y-3 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
              <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
              <span>
                <code class="font-mono">systemctl status emisar</code>
                — check the service is running. If it is not, start it and read the error.
              </span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
              <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
              <span>
                <code class="font-mono">sudo journalctl -u emisar -n 200</code>
                — the log shows what failed. A <code class="font-mono">401</code>
                means the enrollment key was already used, expired, or revoked. Create a new key
                and run the installer again.
              </span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
              <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
              <span>
                A <code class="font-mono">409</code>
                means another runner already holds this name. A runner's name defaults to its
                hostname and cannot be renamed, so this is what a rebuilt host or a restored
                image looks like. Delete the existing one under
                <a
                  href={~p"/app/runners"}
                  class="font-medium text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/30 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
                >Runners</a>
                to free the name, or set <code class="font-mono">runner.id</code>
                in this host's config to register under a declared name.
              </span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
              <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
              <span>
                A <code class="font-mono">402</code>
                means the account is at its plan's runner limit — three on the free plan. Delete a
                runner you no longer use, or move to a larger plan.
              </span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
              <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
              <span>
                A connect that hangs instead of failing is a blocked path, not a bad key. Allow
                outbound HTTPS to <code class="font-mono">emisar.dev:443</code>.
              </span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
              <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
              <span>
                <code class="font-mono">sudo emisar doctor</code>
                — checks the config, credentials, packs, and action binaries in one pass.
              </span>
            </li>
          </ul>
          <p class="mt-3 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
            More symptoms and fixes: <.link
              navigate={~p"/docs/troubleshooting"}
              class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
            >Troubleshooting</.link>.
          </p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <.docs_h2 id="first-action">4. Run your first action</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Open the runner you just added from <a
          href={~p"/app/runners"}
          class="font-medium text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/30 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
        >Runners</a>. Select the <code class="font-mono text-brand-300">linux.uptime</code>
        action. Add a short <strong>reason</strong>, then click <strong>Dispatch</strong>. The output streams back:
      </p>
      <.docs_code phx-no-format label="output"> 14:32:07 up 18 days,  4:01,  load average: 0.42, 0.51, 0.48</.docs_code>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <code class="font-mono">linux.uptime</code>
        is <.docs_risk tier="low" /> risk, so the default policy runs it without approval. A
        <.docs_risk tier="high" />-risk action would stop for human approval instead. You set what each risk tier does
        in your policy. The cloud
        audit and runner journal record the run and its reason. See
        <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/run-an-action"}
          class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
        >Run an action</.link>
        for the full console workflow.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="connect-llm">5. Point your LLM at it</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        This is the step everything above was for: connect your AI agent, and the catalog becomes
        tools it can call — reads run at once, risky work waits for your approval. In the
        console, open
        <a
          href={~p"/app/agents"}
          class="font-medium text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/30 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
        >AI agents</a>
        and select your client. Claude.ai and ChatGPT connect over remote MCP with OAuth, and the page walks you through it. Local and CLI clients use the
        <code>emisar-mcp</code>
        bridge. Install it on the machine where your client runs:
      </p>
      <.os_docs_code phx-no-format detected={@detected_os}>
        <:tab
          os={:linux}
          label="Linux"
          copy_text="curl -fsSL https://emisar.dev/install-mcp.sh | sudo bash"
        ><span class="text-zinc-500">$</span> curl -fsSL <span class="text-brand-300">https://emisar.dev/install-mcp.sh</span> | sudo bash</:tab>
        <:tab
          os={:windows}
          label="Windows"
          copy_text="irm https://emisar.dev/install-mcp.ps1 | iex"
        ><span class="text-zinc-500">PS&gt;</span> irm <span class="text-brand-300">https://emisar.dev/install-mcp.ps1</span> | iex</:tab>
        <:tab
          os={:macos}
          label="macOS"
          copy_text="curl -fsSL https://emisar.dev/install-mcp.sh | sudo bash"
        ><span class="text-zinc-500">$</span> curl -fsSL <span class="text-brand-300">https://emisar.dev/install-mcp.sh</span> | sudo bash</:tab>
      </.os_docs_code>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        The installer finds supported clients, opens a browser approval, and adds emisar to each
        client you select. For manual setup, see <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/connect-cli-agent"}
          class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
        >Connect a local AI client</.link>.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Restart your AI client, then ask it
        <em>"list my emisar runners and check the load on each one."</em>
        You are done when it returns the result from <code class="font-mono">linux.uptime</code>
        and the run appears in <a
          href={~p"/app/runs"}
          class="font-medium text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/30 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
        >Runs</a>.
      </p>

      <%!-- Optional, secondary to step 5: most clients prompt "allow this tool?"
           per call. Because emisar gates server-side, that client prompt is
           redundant for emisar's tools and safe to silence — the framing the
           agents page uses, repeated here for the public docs/SEO surface. --%>
      <details class="group mt-6 overflow-hidden rounded-lg border border-zinc-900 bg-black/40">
        <summary class="flex cursor-pointer items-center justify-between gap-4 px-5 py-4 text-sm font-semibold text-zinc-100 transition-colors hover:bg-zinc-900/30 [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">
          <span class="flex items-center gap-2">
            <.icon name="communication.prompt_suppressed" class="h-4 w-4 text-zinc-500" />
            Stop your AI client asking permission for every emisar action
          </span>
          <.icon
            name="action.disclose"
            class="h-5 w-5 shrink-0 text-zinc-500 transition duration-200 group-hover:text-zinc-300 group-open:rotate-180 group-open:text-brand-400"
          />
        </summary>
        <div class="border-t border-zinc-900 px-5 pb-5 pt-4">
          <p class="text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
            Most clients ask <em>"allow this tool?"</em>
            the first time a model calls one. That prompt is redundant for emisar's tools, because
            emisar gates every action <strong class="text-zinc-200">server-side</strong>: account
            policy applies to every call, risky actions wait for human approval, and out-of-policy
            ones are denied.
          </p>
          <p class="mt-3 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
            The installer offers to do this for Claude Code, Gemini CLI, Codex CLI, and Grok CLI —
            the four clients whose setting can name the
            <code class="font-mono text-brand-300">emisar</code>
            server alone. It leaves Cursor alone, because Cursor's only control is global.
            Whichever way you answer, auto-permitting in a client never bypasses emisar's gate. To
            set it yourself:
          </p>
          <ul class="mt-3 space-y-2 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
              <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
              <span>
                <strong class="text-zinc-300">Claude Code</strong>
                — add <code class="font-mono text-brand-300">"mcp__emisar__*"</code>
                to <code class="font-mono">permissions.allow</code>
                in <code class="font-mono">~/.claude/settings.json</code>.
              </span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
              <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
              <span>
                <strong class="text-zinc-300">Gemini CLI</strong>
                — set <code class="font-mono text-brand-300">"trust": true</code>
                on the <code class="font-mono">emisar</code>
                server in <code class="font-mono">~/.gemini/settings.json</code>.
              </span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
              <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
              <span>
                <strong class="text-zinc-300">Grok CLI</strong>
                — add <code class="font-mono text-brand-300">"MCPTool(emisar__*)"</code>
                to <code class="font-mono">[permission].allow</code>
                in <code class="font-mono">~/.grok/config.toml</code>.
              </span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
              <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
              <span>
                <strong class="text-zinc-300">Cursor, Codex CLI</strong>
                — Cursor approves globally through agent auto-run. Codex can trust only emisar with
                <code class="font-mono">default_tools_approval_mode = "approve"</code>
                under <code class="font-mono">[mcp_servers.emisar]</code>. The
                <.link navigate={~p"/app/agents"} class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300">
                  AI agents
                </.link>
                page has the exact per-client steps.
              </span>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </details>

      <.docs_h2 id="workflow">6. The workflow we recommend</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        emisar gives an agent eyes and hands on production, restrained by the policy you set. It can make
        changes — that is what a gated action is for — but don't use it as your configuration
        manager. Standing changes an agent makes on a live host are how you get drift nobody can
        reproduce.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        So the durable pattern pairs the agent with your infrastructure-as-code:
      </p>
      <ol class="mt-4 list-decimal space-y-3 pl-6 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <li>
          The agent <strong class="text-zinc-200">investigates</strong>
          through emisar and finds the root cause — reads run on policy, so this part is unattended
          and on the record.
        </li>
        <li>
          If it's on fire, it <strong class="text-zinc-200">hot-patches</strong>
          through one gated action to contain it — a person approves, and the side effect waits
          until they do.
        </li>
        <li>
          Then it <strong class="text-zinc-200">writes the durable fix as code</strong>
          — Terraform, Ansible, a Kubernetes manifest, whatever your fleet runs — and hands you a
          change you <strong class="text-zinc-200">review and apply</strong>
          through your normal pipeline. The hot-patch bought time; this is the part that survives
          the next converge.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        And cover as much of your infrastructure as you can: the more actions an agent can
        reach, the more it can do for you, and breadth is safe because policy decides what
        actually runs. Adding runners and matching packs to each host is in <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/runner-fleet"}
          class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
        >Manage the runner fleet</.link>.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        See it end to end on a real incident: <.link
          navigate={~p"/use-cases/csi-data-loss"}
          class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
        >the 33-hour wipe</.link>.
      </p>

      <.docs_callout kind={:tip} title="What you just set up">
        <p>
          There is now a finite, reviewed catalog between an AI agent and your host: the model can
          call only declared actions, risky calls wait for a person, and configured patterns
          redact matching output on the host. The audit records who ran what, and why.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-3">
          Next,
          <.link
            navigate={~p"/docs/use-a-published-pack"}
            class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
          >add a published pack</.link>
          for a service you operate.
        </p>
      </.docs_callout>
    </.docs_layout>
  </section>

  <.marketing_footer />
</div>
